ROLE
You are a senior Java engineer building "upyog-mcp-server": an MCP (Model Context Protocol)
server that lets the UPYOG assistant and other AI agents perform citizen/employee tasks on
UPYOG (DIGIT-based). It is a thin, secure, DESCRIPTOR-DRIVEN orchestration layer. All
business rules stay in the existing UPYOG services.

DESIGN PRINCIPLE
Do NOT create one MCP tool per REST endpoint. Expose a small fixed set of generic tools
(below). Module-specific knowledge lives in per-service descriptor files, so adding a new
module (Trade License, Water & Sewerage, ...) means adding a descriptor, not writing tools.

STEP 0 - DISCOVER BEFORE CODING
I will provide API specs for: pgr-services, property-services, billing-service,
advertisement-service and the community hall (venue) booking service. Read them plus the
UPYOG repo/MDMS config and produce a short discovery report: for each operation, the exact
endpoint, request/response models, required fields, workflow business service and action
names, MDMS masters, and role-actions needed. Also document: how the Zuul gateway
authenticates and injects user info into RequestInfo, how egov-accesscontrol authorizes,
how egov-user validates a token, and which org.egov.tracer version is compatible with our
Spring Boot version. Flag ambiguity instead of guessing. Wait for my review before
implementing write operations.

STACK
Java 17, Spring Boot 3.2.x, Maven. Spring AI MCP server starter (WebMVC) or the official
MCP Java SDK (verify Streamable HTTP support; stdio only as a dev profile). RestClient,
Resilience4j, Micrometer + OpenTelemetry, structured JSON logs, Caffeine, Bean Validation,
Jackson + JsonPath for descriptor templating, JSON Schema validation, WireMock + JUnit 5.
Stateless: no in-memory session state.

REUSE EXISTING UPYOG INFRASTRUCTURE
1. Every downstream call goes THROUGH THE GATEWAY with the end user's auth-token. The
   gateway does authentication and RBAC via egov-accesscontrol. No shared superuser token.
   Do not build your own permission model (tool-list filtering is UX only).
2. Validate the Bearer token at the MCP edge via egov-user; read uuid, roles, tenant.
   Login/OTP is not this server's job.
3. Build RequestInfo server-side (apiId, ver, ts, msgId, authToken, userInfo, correlationId).
   The agent can never supply user identity or RequestInfo. tenantId may be an argument but
   must be validated against the user's allowed tenants.
4. Tracing: use org.egov.tracer if compatible, otherwise replicate its correlation-ID
   behaviour. Propagate the correlation ID on every downstream call, log line and audit
   event.
5. Reuse egov-mdms-service, egov-localization, egov-workflow-v2, billing-service.

GENERIC TOOLS (fixed set)
- list_services: services and operations the caller may use (role-filtered).
- describe_operation(service, operation): returns the input JSON schema, required fields,
  allowed filters, examples, and whether confirmation is required.
- lookup_master(name, filters): read-only MDMS masters from an allow-list (complaint types,
  venues, ad spaces, etc.), cached.
- search(service, filters, page, size): read-only; filters validated against the descriptor.
- get_status(service, id): status, last action, key timeline, SLA if available.
- prepare_action(service, operation, payload, idempotencyKey): validates against the
  descriptor schema, returns a human-readable summary of exactly what will happen plus a
  confirmationToken. Executes nothing.
- confirm_action(confirmationToken): executes the prepared action.
- get_pending_bill(businessService, consumerCode, tenantId): fetch pending bill via
  billing-service; explicit result when nothing is due.
- get_payment_link(businessService, consumerCode, tenantId): verify a payable bill exists,
  then return a direct link built from a CONFIGURABLE URL template (placeholders for
  consumerCode, tenantId, businessService). NEVER initiate, complete or store payments.
  Confirm the real citizen pay-page path from the UI code.
Each tool has MCP annotations (readOnlyHint / destructiveHint / idempotentHint). Keep
descriptions precise so the LLM picks the right tool.

PHASE 1 SERVICES (descriptors to write)
- pgr: operations create (complaint), search, status.
- property: operations search (pending bill and payment link use the generic bill tools with
  the PT business service).
- advertisement (advertisement-service): operations create (booking), search.
- venue-booking (community hall booking service): operations create, search, cancel.
  Cancel is destructive: the prepare step must show what the backend says about
  cancellation/refund terms; never compute refunds locally.
If any create flow needs document upload, do not handle files in v1: return a clear message
with a deep link to the UI for that step.

SERVICE DESCRIPTOR (YAML/JSON, one per service, validated at startup)
Each descriptor declares: id, displayName, description (for the LLM), allowedRoles hint,
and per operation:
- type (search | status | create | cancel | update), read-only/destructive flags,
  requiresConfirmation
- downstream method + gateway path, request template (with placeholders for RequestInfo,
  tenantId, and payload fields), and pagination mapping
- flattened, LLM-friendly input JSON schema (additionalProperties=false, enums, examples)
- friendly-filter to downstream-param mapping
- response projection (fields returned), id field, status field, timeline source
- PII masking rules (JSONPath + mask type)
- billing businessService and workflow business service where relevant
- role-actions URLs this operation needs in MDMS (used to generate a checklist)
Rules: the agent chooses only registry service/operation names. It never supplies a URL,
HTTP method, or raw request body. Unknown fields are rejected. Provide a Java
ServiceAdapter interface as an escape hatch for operations the template engine cannot
express (for example cancel implemented as _update with a workflow action). Provide a
tool to lint descriptors and a startup failure if one is invalid. Optionally, behind a
disabled-by-default feature flag, generate per-operation tools from descriptors.

WRITE SAFETY
- prepare_action never writes. confirm_action executes only with a valid token.
- confirmationToken is HMAC-signed and binds userId + service + operation + hash of the
  payload + expiry (default 5 min). Single use via Redis if available, otherwise short TTL
  plus idempotency key.
- Idempotency key on every create/cancel, passed downstream where supported and
  de-duplicated locally otherwise.
- Tool descriptions must instruct the agent to obtain explicit user confirmation before
  calling confirm_action.

DATA PROTECTION AND SAFETY
- Mask PII (mobile, Aadhaar, email, full address where not needed, owner names in
  searches) in output and never log bodies containing PII.
- Free text from citizens/employees (complaint descriptions, remarks, comments) is untrusted:
  return it in a clearly delimited data field and never let it influence tool behaviour.
  Never echo tokens or internal URLs.
- Follow DPDP principles: minimisation, purpose limitation, retention limits on audit logs.

RELIABILITY AND LIMITS
- Per-operation timeouts (reads about 8s, writes about 20s), retries only for idempotent
  reads, circuit breaker per downstream, per-client and per-user rate limits.
- Hard caps on page size, response size and input length.
- Uniform structured errors: code, message, retryable flag, suggested next step. No stack
  traces or raw downstream bodies.
- Audit event for every call: user uuid, client id, service, operation, tenant, redacted
  args, outcome, latency, correlation ID.
- Health/readiness endpoints, graceful shutdown, config via env/ConfigMap, Dockerfile and
  Helm/K8s manifests consistent with other UPYOG services.

NON-GOALS (v1)
File uploads, payment initiation or completion, login/OTP, employee approval/workflow
actions, refund calculation, duplicating any business rule from UPYOG services.

